ldm (3.3.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The greeter's date is no longer ambiguous. The clock used %x, the
    locale's own date format, which is only as good as the locale the image
    happens to carry - a stock thin client image is en_US, so a greeter
    standing in Germany showed 09/17/2026. The problem is not that it is
    foreign but that it cannot be read with certainty: 09/10 is the 9th of
    October or the 10th of September depending on who is looking, and
    nothing on the screen says which.

    The default is now ISO 8601, "%Y-%m-%d, <b>%H:%M</b>", which has one
    reading everywhere. A site that wants its own convention sets
    LDM_CLOCK_FORMAT in lts.conf and gets it whatever the image's locale
    is - "%d.%m.%Y, <b>%H:%M</b>" for the German form. Documented in
    lts.conf(5).

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <butz@labor-heidrich.de>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 16:50:35 +0200

ldm (3.3.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * RDP_PREAUTH=ssh now reaches a verdict. 3.3.2 fixed half of it: the
    password was finally sent and sshd logged the failure, but the answer
    was still thrown away and the login still went through.

    ldm_pty_expect() asked "has the child died?" before comparing what it
    had just read. For a session that is the right order - the ssh backend
    wants to hear about a client that dies mid-session. For a one-shot
    question it is exactly wrong, because the answer and the death arrive
    together: ssh prints

        Permission denied (publickey,password).

    and exits in the same breath. The string was sitting in the buffer, and
    the function reported LDM_PTY_ERROR anyway - which the pre-auth reads as
    "could not ask", and a question that could not be asked counts against
    nobody.

    The pattern comparison now happens first, and a match is returned
    whatever the child does afterwards. A child that dies without answering
    is still an error, and silence is still a timeout.

    Verified against a real sshd rather than a stand-in, which is why this
    took three releases to find: a scripted stand-in stays alive after
    speaking, and ssh does not. The harness drives the real
    ldm_pty_expect() through the same sequence the plugin uses, with the
    same ssh-keygen call in front of it, and distinguishes all three states
    - 3.3.1 never sends the password, 3.3.2 sends it and discards the
    reply, and this one rejects a wrong password, accepts a right one, and
    still reports an unreachable server as unreachable.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <butz@labor-heidrich.de>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 16:15:34 +0200

ldm (3.3.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * RDP_PREAUTH=ssh actually decides again. Since 3.3.0 it declined to, on
    every login, and a declined check lets the login through - so a wrong
    password reached the RDP client exactly as if no check were configured.
    The log said

        rdp_preauth_ssh: inconclusive (-1), skipping:

    with an empty buffer, while the sshd on the other end recorded a
    connection that closed before any password arrived.

    The cause is a sticky flag. child_exited is one global, set by ldm's
    SIGCHLD handler for *any* child and cleared by nobody, and
    ldm_pty_expect() reads it as "the child we are talking to has died" -
    returning LDM_PTY_ERROR whatever it had just read. 3.3.0 added a host
    key lookup that runs ssh-keygen -F immediately before the probe, so by
    the time ssh had its pty the flag was already up, and the answer was
    discarded before the patterns were even compared.

    ldm_pty_spawn() now clears it before forking. A test harness drives the
    real ldm_pty_expect() over a pty and covers both directions: with the
    flag pre-set, a password prompt and a "Permission denied" line are now
    recognised where the old code returned -1 on both; a child that dies
    without saying anything is still an error, and silence is still a
    timeout.

    This is the last of three faults between a wrong password and the
    message saying so, and the only one that could not be seen from the
    outside: 3.3.0 refused every login when no host key was configured,
    3.3.1 read "publickey" in a method list as "no password was offered",
    and this one threw the answer away after reading it correctly.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <butz@labor-heidrich.de>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 15:55:08 +0200

ldm (3.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A wrong user name or password under RDP_PREAUTH=ssh now says so. It
    said nothing at all: the greeter simply came back, with no hint that
    anything had been rejected, which reads as a hang rather than a refusal.

    The pre-auth check runs ssh against the session server and reads its
    refusal. ssh reports one as

        Permission denied (publickey,password).

    and the parenthesised list is the methods the server was willing to try.
    Two very different outcomes land on that line and only the list tells
    them apart: "(publickey)" means no password was ever offered, so nothing
    was tested and there is nothing to report, while "(publickey,password)"
    means a password was sent and refused. The check looked for the word
    "publickey" anywhere in the output - and it appears in both. So every
    wrong password was read as "this server does not do passwords", the
    check skipped itself, and the user was told nothing.

    It now reads the method list rather than the line, and treats an
    unparseable refusal as a real one, on the grounds that a refusal without
    a method list is more likely a rejected password than a server policy.
    Covered by a ten-case harness over real ssh refusal strings, including
    keyboard-interactive, reversed method order, and the word "password"
    appearing in the text outside the list.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <butz@labor-heidrich.de>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 14:56:21 +0200

ldm (3.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * RDP_PREAUTH=ssh no longer refuses every login when no host key is
    configured for the server it would ask. It now looks for an entry for
    *that host* rather than merely checking that ssh_known_hosts exists and
    is non-empty, and skips the check - loudly, naming the host and the file
    - when there is none.

    The old test made a set of foreign keys worse than no keys at all. An
    image ships with whatever ssh_known_hosts was baked into it, and those
    keys belong to the lab it was built in. Deploy that image anywhere else
    and the file is present, non-empty and completely irrelevant: the check
    called itself usable, probed a server whose key it did not have, and the
    unknown-key branch refused the login. Every login, with the message "The
    session server could not be verified", which points at the wrong problem
    entirely - nobody had misconfigured anything except the absence of a
    configuration line.

    The security property is unchanged where it matters. A host key that is
    present and does not match still refuses, because that is the case the
    check exists to catch. What changed is only the case of no entry at all,
    which is a gap rather than an attack, and where the honest answer is to
    say so and leave the login exactly as it would be with RDP_PREAUTH unset.

    ssh-keygen -F does the lookup, so hashed known_hosts entries work too.

  * The automatic path (RDP_PREAUTH=True without a Kerberos realm) uses the
    same per-host test, so it no longer selects the ssh probe on the strength
    of an unrelated file.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 12:50:00 +0200

ldm (3.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: the greeter can warn about a lockout before it happens, and say
    how long it lasts once it has. Sites protect RDP with fail2ban, so a
    few wrong passwords block the client at the network level for some
    minutes; from the seat that is baffling, because the screen that
    worked a moment ago now fails in some entirely different way, and
    nothing can be learned by asking the server - being unable to reach
    the server is the state in question.

    What ldm can honestly observe was measured rather than assumed.
    Against xrdp a rejected password makes the RDP client neither exit nor
    report anything: the session stays open showing xrdp's own login
    window, and the retries happen in there, invisible to ldm. Failed RDP
    logins are therefore not countable from the client at all. The
    optional pre-authentication is the one place a rejection is visible,
    and it is exactly where the counting now happens.

    Those rejections are worth counting even though a rejected pre-auth
    never reaches the RDP server, because they reach something else that
    counts them: RDP_PREAUTH=ssh leaves a failed password in the sshd log,
    where an sshd jail bans the client (on every port, with
    banaction_allports); RDP_PREAUTH=krb5 raises badPwdCount on the domain
    controller, where the account lockout policy is counting.

    New lts.conf settings, all optional:
      - RDP_PREAUTH_MAXFAIL    rejections before the client is considered
                               blocked. Off by default, because the right
                               number is whatever the site configured on
                               the server and a warning about a lockout
                               that does not exist is worse than silence.
      - RDP_PREAUTH_FAILWINDOW seconds over which they are counted (600).
      - RDP_PREAUTH_LOCKTIME   how long the block lasts, for the countdown
                               the greeter shows (600).

    The tally is a file under /run/ldm/lockout and is therefore shared by
    every screen on the client. That is the point rather than an
    implementation detail: SCREEN_05 through SCREEN_08 are four greeters
    behind one address, and the thing doing the banning counts per
    address, so a per-process counter would see a quarter of what the
    server sees. It lives in tmpfs, so a reboot forgets it - the greeter
    then says nothing rather than guessing, which is the honest failure
    mode for a warning.

  * The pre-authentication backends now report what they established, not
    just a message to display. "Wrong user name or password." and "Cannot
    reach the authentication server." were both simply a non-NULL return,
    and only the first is an attempt anyone counted; telling them apart by
    inspecting a translated string would not be a protocol. An account
    that is already locked or whose password expired is reported as barred
    rather than rejected, because neither is a failed attempt.

  * New file ldmlockout.c, built into the ldm binary alongside ldmpty.c.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:05:00 +0200

ldm (3.1.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Rebuilt so that screen.d/ldm keeps the executable bit git records for
    it. Same cause as ltsp 6.1.0-2: a Windows checkout has core.filemode
    false, so the build tree carries no executable bits, and the packaging
    copies that tree as it finds it. The screen script is what
    screen_session executes for SCREEN_nn=ldm, so without the bit the
    greeter cannot start even once everything else is right.

