ldm (3.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: RDP_PREAUTH=ssh, for sites with local accounts and no domain. The
    Kerberos pre-authentication added in 3.0.2 answers the same question -
    are these credentials good, asked before handing them to a client that
    cannot report a rejection - but it needs a KDC. RDP_PREAUTH=ssh asks an
    sshd instead, which also means it tests the PAM stack of the machine
    that will run the session rather than just the password: an expired
    account, a pam_access rule or an sssd access rule all deny here too.
  * RDP_PREAUTH=True now means "check", not "check with Kerberos", and
    picks the method: a configured realm (RDP_PREAUTH_REALM, or
    default_realm in krb5.conf) means a domain worth asking, and without
    one the ssh probe is the only thing that can answer. RDP_PREAUTH=krb5
    and RDP_PREAUTH=ssh still force one outright, and False still means no
    check at all, exactly as before.
  * The automatic fallback to ssh is gated on the host keys actually being
    present, and only the automatic one. The ssh probe refuses on an
    unverified host key and that refusal deliberately does not fail open -
    so on a client with an empty ssh_known_hosts it would reject every
    login. Reaching that by upgrading, with RDP_PREAUTH=True quietly
    acquiring a second meaning, would be a lockout nobody asked for. An
    explicit RDP_PREAUTH=ssh is not gated: being told plainly that it is
    not set up is different from having it chosen on your behalf.
  * RDP_PREAUTH_HOST, optional. The check normally goes to RDP_SERVER; set
    this only when RDP_SERVER is a load balancer, since HAProxy forwards
    3389 and an ssh connection to that address would reach the balancer
    rather than a session server.
  * The pty machinery moved from the ssh backend into src/ldmpty.c, shared
    by both plugins through the ldm binary's dynamic exports, the same way
    they already share ldm_wait() and child_exited(). expect() was moved
    verbatim rather than reimplemented; the ssh backend keeps opening its
    own pty, because its session outlives the one-shot exchange the new
    check needs.
  * Security, and the reason this is not simply the better option: unlike
    Kerberos, which never puts the password on the wire, the ssh probe
    sends it to whatever answers on that address. A thin client booting a
    read-only image has no persistent known_hosts, so trust-on-first-use
    never gets past "first use". StrictHostKeyChecking stays on and the
    host keys belong in the image - which is what ltsp-update-sshkeys(8)
    has always done for the X11-over-ssh backend, and it copies public
    keys only. Verified against OpenSSH: with an unknown host key ssh
    refuses before it asks for a password, so nothing is disclosed.
  * The probe fails open where the question could not be asked - no host
    configured, ssh missing, or a server refusing password authentication
    outright ("Permission denied (publickey)") - and deliberately does not
    fail open on host key verification, which is the case it exists to
    catch.

  * Numbered 3.1.0 rather than 3.0.3: a second authentication method and a
    changed meaning for an existing lts.conf directive are not a patch-level
    matter, and the version is what an administrator reads before deciding
    whether an upgrade needs thinking about.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 22:30:00 +0200

ldm (3.0.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Merge ldm-graphical-session-fixes: KDC pre-authentication for the RDP
    backend, a reason shown to the user when an RDP session ends, the
    Preferences menu anchored so it opens away from the screen edge, a
    plugin directory that no longer has to sit where LDM_PLUG_DIR points,
    and LF line endings enforced via .gitattributes.
  * ldm.service now runs screen_session 07 rather than the xinit line that
    3.0.1-2 introduced. Both fixed the same fault - the unit started no X
    server - but screen_session is the better of the two: it sets up the
    xauth cookie and takes the X arguments from lts.conf (X_COLOR_DEPTH
    among them), which a bare xinit call does not, and it matches the
    SCREEN_07 default that ltsp_config.d/15default-screen already sets,
    including SCREEN_07_WAIT_FGCONSOLE=False.
  * The unit is guarded with ConditionPathExists on
    /usr/share/ltsp/screen_session. That path belongs to ltsp-client-core,
    which this package does not depend on, so without the guard the unit
    fails on every host that has ldm without LTSP.

